fix(deploy): make the production topology actually buildable and runnable

The repo could not be deployed as it stood. These are the defects that
blocked it, all now fixed and verified.

Blocking:
* cmd/server/routes.go and internal/a2a/server/pathvalue.go were never
  committed. The .gitignore pattern "server" (unanchored) matched every
  directory named server at any depth, so git silently refused to add NEW
  files under cmd/server/ and internal/a2a/server/. main.go calls
  newServeMux(), which only exists in routes.go, so a fresh clone of develop
  did not compile. Anchored the pattern and committed both files.
* web/server/ (the Nitro proxy layer) was ignored by the same rule, and the
  /api proxy route did not exist at all. Added server/routes/api/[...path].ts
  and server/routes/healthz.ts; server/routes/ws.ts existed on disk but was
  untracked.
* web/Dockerfile pinned node:20 while Nuxt 4.5 needs the trustedFunctions
  global (Node >= 22.12). The image build failed with
  "TypeError: trustedFunctions.difference is not a function". Bumped to
  node:22 and raised the engines floor to match.
* Root Dockerfile built the frontend into web/dist, which Nuxt 4 SSR does not
  produce, and used golang:1.24 against a go 1.26 module. Rewritten as a
  Go-only build (golang:1.27, non-root, static binary, migrations, healthcheck).
* docker-compose published the Go service on a host port and pointed the
  browser at the internal hostname mcp-server. Rewritten as a single-origin
  stack: Nuxt SSR (:3000) is the only public port, /api and /ws are proxied
  server-side, and migrations run as a one-shot job gated by
  service_completed_successfully.

Correctness:
* ws.ts used peer.on(), which does not exist on crossws Peer. The proxy threw
  on the first client frame. Rewritten against the real hook API
  (open/message/close) with upstream cleanup in the close hook.
* useRealtime connect() took a parameter named projectId, shadowing the ref, so
  projectId.value was written to a string primitive.
* layouts/default.vue destructured url from useApi(), which does not expose it;
  the watcher was dead and url.value would throw on a failed health check.
* useToastBus derived ToastInput via Parameters<ReturnType<typeof useToast>>,
  which collapses to unknown. It also passed "timeout", renamed to "duration"
  in Nuxt UI v3, so the intended 4s was silently ignored.
* useServerUrl hardcoded localhost:8080 as the default, which breaks any
  real deployment. Empty now means same-origin, routed through the proxy.
* isTLS was hardcoded false, so HSTS was never advertised. Driven by
  TLS_TERMINATED.
* The production sslmode=disable guard also fired on private hosts, which
  made the documented compose topology unbootable. Now scoped to public hosts,
  with a table test.
* The SPA catch-all logged on every unmatched request.

Infrastructure:
* Added .gitattributes pinning text to LF. Without it a Windows checkout
  rewrote 50+ Go files to CRLF and gofmt -l flagged the entire tree.
* Added .dockerignore at the root and in web/; the build context was shipping
  node_modules.
* ci.yml triggered only on main, ran a duplicate "env:" key that clobbered the
  Postgres credentials, tested a -tags=integration build with no tagged tests,
  pinned golangci-lint v1.61 against a Go 1.26 module, and never built the
  frontend. Rewritten: develop trigger, frontend and container jobs, v2 lint.
* Untracked web/_out_old/ (70+ stale build artefacts), 15 dot-prefixed .ps1
  helpers, and bin/*.log.
* .env.example documented 2 of ~15 env vars. Completed.
* Makefile: lint swallowed its own failures with || true, air referenced a
  missing .air.toml, and there were no frontend targets. Rewritten.

Verified: gofmt -l clean, go build ./... 0, go vet ./... 0, go test ./... 5/5
packages, nuxt typecheck exit 0, both Docker images build, docker compose config
valid.
